Reflow a line taller than the screen whole, and keep its cursor

Resizing with reflow on lost text and could abort the process. Five
faults, all in resize_buffer() and its caller, and all reached once a
line wraps over more rows than the screen has, which a small window
makes ordinary:

- A line that no longer fit above the rows already placed was pushed to
  the scrollback by its first row only; every row after it was dropped.
  With the cursor in that line, no position was found for it and the
  process aborted ("screen_resize failed to update cursor position").
  The line is split now: its tail fills the top of the screen and its
  head goes to the scrollback, rewrapped at the new width.

- A top row that continues a line whose head is in the scrollback made
  the walk back over continuations run to row -1 and read before the
  buffer. With sb_popline4 (below) the head is popped back first and the
  whole line reflowed as one; without it the top row starts a line and
  keeps its continuation mark.

- Rows backfilled from the scrollback were asked for at the old width,
  losing what lay past it when the screen grew, and kept whatever the
  allocator left in their line info. They are asked for at the new width
  and their line info is zeroed, or set from sb_popline4.

- Cursor just past text that ends exactly at the new edge was clamped
  onto the last character with no pending wrap, so the next character
  overwrote it. The pending wrap is carried in VTermStateFields and set
  there, and on_resize no longer cancels it when the cursor moves.

- sb_popline4, beside sb_pushline4 and enabled by the same opt-in, tells
  the caller's scrollback whether a line it hands back continues the one
  before it, so a wrapped line brought back by a resize is marked as one
  line again rather than rewrapped as pieces.

